<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Luthuli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1898–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Luthuli was a South African anti-apartheid activist and traditional leader who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> from 1952. Elected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> chief at Groutville in 1935, he was deposed by the government in 1952 for refusing to choose between the chieftaincy and the ANC. Repeatedly banned, he led non-violent resistance inspired by his Christian faith and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, and received the 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1961. The official account was that a train struck him in 1967, but in October 2025 a reopened inquest ruled that apartheid police had beaten him to death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli.jpg" alt="Albert Luthuli" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Lutuli_Anefo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
